"χ² = Σ (O − E)²/E over every cell. Goodness of fit: E = n·p for each category. Independence and homogeneity: E = (row total)(column total)/(grand total). Single variance: χ² = (n − {#})s²/σ₀². The p value is the area under the chi square curve beyond the statistic (demos/lib/vdstats.js, the regularized incomplete gamma function), as the book's calculator gives it.",
"The chi square curve is an approximation for counts: it is good when every expected count is at least {#} (book {#}), which is why small cells are flagged and the decay counts are grouped. The single variance test needs the data to be normal.",
"The simulation needs no approximation. Goodness of fit: n items fall into the categories with the hypothesized chances. Decay counts: n Poisson counts with the estimated mean, regrouped in the same bins with their own estimated mean. Independence: the grand total falls into the cells with chances (row share)(column share). Homogeneity: each row keeps its own total and falls into the columns with the pooled column shares. Single variance: n normal values with σ = σ₀. The simulated p value is the share of simulated statistics at or beyond the observed one.",
